Ubuntu 24.04 LTS: The foundation for everything. Why is it everywhere?

From AWS clouds to home NAS setups, from desktops to containers — Ubuntu dominates. It’s more than just an OS; it’s an ecosystem featuring Long-Term Support (LTS), built-in security, and a massive community. Let’s explore why it has become the de facto standard.

1️⃣ Kernel and support: stability for 5+ years
# Check your version and kernel
lsb_release -a
uname -r

Each LTS release (22.04, 24.04) is supported for 5 years with security and critical updates. The kernel is kept up-to-date, with fixes backported from newer versions.

2️⃣ Security out of the box: AppArmor and unattended-upgrades
# Check the status of AppArmor profiles
sudo apparmor_status
# Configure automatic security updates
sudo dpkg-reconfigure unattended-upgrades

AppArmor restricts application capabilities by default. Unattended-upgrades automatically installs security patches, meaning the server does not require constant manual attention.

3️⃣ Package management: APT and Snap
# Classic APT for stability
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade
# Snap for isolated, up-to-date versions
sudo snap install code --classic

Two worlds in one system: reliable, verified packages from Ubuntu repositories alongside modern, self-contained Snap applications with automatic updates.

4️⃣ Optimization for clouds and containers
# Minimal image for Docker
FROM ubuntu:24.04
# Check pre-installed cloud-init metadata
sudo cloud-init status


Official Ubuntu images serve as the foundation for most Docker containers and cloud-based virtual machines. Integration with `cloud-init` allows for the automated configuration of instances upon startup.

5️⃣ Performance and Tuning
# Switch to the fast systemd-boot bootloader (for pure UEFI systems)
sudo bootctl install
# Install a low-level system tuner
sudo apt install tuned && sudo tuned-adm profile throughput-performance

The default configuration is well-balanced, but simple tools are available for fine-tuning to suit specialized workloads (such as databases or media processing).

6️⃣ Debugging and Monitoring
# View system logs
sudo journalctl -f -u ssh
# Install and launch a resource monitor
sudo apt install btop && sudo btop

All system services log to a unified `journald` system. A wide range of console-based utilities is available for instant diagnostics.

Quick Start: Deploy a server in just 5 commands



Updates and basic security:
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install ufw fail2ban -y
sudo ufw allow 22/tcp && sudo ufw enable

Installing the LEMP stack (Nginx, MySQL, PHP):
sudo apt install nginx mysql-server php-fpm php-mysql -y
sudo systemctl enable --now nginx mysql

✅ Functionality check
systemctl status nginx mysql
curl -I localhost

Open your browser and enter the server's IP address — you will see the Nginx page.

🔄 Update channels: from stable to cutting-edge

Keep your production environment on standard repositories. For testing new software versions, use PPAs or backports:
sudo add-apt-repository ppa:deadsnakes/ppa  # Example: newer versions of Python


sudo apt update && sudo apt install python3.13

🚨When might you not want Ubuntu?
- You need absolute minimalism: Alpine Linux.

- You require strict control without systemd: Devuan or Void.

- You need maximum stability with no changes: Debian Stable.

💥Bottom line: a versatile, secure, and well-documented foundation for 95% of tasks. Free of charge.